### The Paradox: Why Constructors for Non-Instantiable Classes?

Beginners often ask:
> *"If I can never write `new AbstractClass()`, why does Java allow or even require constructors in an abstract class?"*

The answer lies in **encapsulation of state**. An abstract class can have instance fields (`private String id`, `protected double balance`). These fields must be initialized when a subclass object is instantiated.

```java
abstract class Entity {
    private final String id;
    public Entity(String id) {
        if (id == null || id.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid id");
        this.id = id; // Encapsulated base state initialization!
    }
    public String getId() { return id; }
}
```

### The Constructor Execution Chain
When you execute `new ConcreteChild("USR-1")`:
1. `ConcreteChild` constructor is invoked.
2. Line 1 calls `super("USR-1")` explicitly (or implicit `super()` if no-arg exists).
3. `AbstractParent` constructor runs, validating and assigning `id`.
4. Control returns to `ConcreteChild` to initialize child fields.

### CRITICAL DANGER: Invoking Overridden Methods in Constructors!
Never invoke an overridable method inside a constructor:
```java
abstract class BadBase {
    public BadBase() {
        init(); // DANGER! Calls child's overridden init() BEFORE child fields are initialized!
    }
    public abstract void init();
}

class Child extends BadBase {
    private String config = "PRODUCTION";
    @Override
    public void init() {
        System.out.println(config.toLowerCase()); // NullPointerException! config is null right now!
    }
}
```
When `BadBase` runs its constructor, `Child.config` has not yet been initialized (it is still `null`), resulting in a crash!

